WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.229
Film Courage:&nbsp; Eric, something you just said
to us off-camera was that when you do see

00:00:03.229 --> 00:00:08.532
students again out in the public arena, they’ll
say “You know, I always remember that you

00:00:08.532 --> 00:00:12.440
encouraged me to write badly with pride”
and how this worked for them.&nbsp;

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.310
Can we talk about that?

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.230
Eric Edson:&nbsp; Yeah.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:18.230
Sure.&nbsp;

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:24.080
Every time I walk into the class, the first
evening (all of our classes are evening classes)

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.869
and in the new class the first year people
are there for the first semester.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:31.429
&nbsp;I walk in and they’re chattering away
and trying to get to know each other and so

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:34.880
forth but they haven’t really had anything
to do with me yet.&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.660
And I walk in.&nbsp;

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.090
It was always weird and creepy because as
soon as I walk in, a deathly silence falls!&nbsp;

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.980
And everybody is waiting.&nbsp;

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:49.450
So I look very austere and serious and I walk
to the white board and I write on the whiteboard

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:57.360
in very, very large letters, ‘WRITE BADLY
WITH PRIDE’ and then they laugh.&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.070
Everybody laughs and it cuts the ice.&nbsp;

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.930
It works that way.&nbsp;

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.490
And I turn around and I smile and I say “Yes!&nbsp;

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:08.689
But I’m not kidding.”

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:16.450
For it is of critical importance for writers
to permanently give themselves permission

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.420
to write badly.&nbsp;

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:29.470
If writer’s block is all about expecting
and demanding of oneself perfection.&nbsp;

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.340
Anything, any word, any sentence, it’s got
to be great.&nbsp;

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.340
It’s got to be great.&nbsp;

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.389
No!&nbsp;

00:01:35.389 --> 00:01:37.439
That way lies writerly death.&nbsp;

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:41.479
You do that and you’ll end up freezing so
solid, you’ll never write again.&nbsp;

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:44.979
It is exactly the opposite.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:46.170
Writing is a craft.&nbsp;

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.210
It’s a craft.&nbsp;

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.340
And how do you participate in a craft.&nbsp;

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.259
It’s a craft exercised with words.&nbsp;

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:03.700
And it is like statuary or something…you
start out with a big gooey pile of wet clay

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.359
and start to mush it.&nbsp;

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:06.749
You get your hands dirty.&nbsp;

00:02:06.749 --> 00:02:11.340
You get down in the mud and you mush it and
squish it and then you start making choices

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.330
as you shape it, the pieces you’re not going
to need and so forth and you go from there.&nbsp;

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.510
But you start out with mess.&nbsp;

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.150
That’s what we do as writers.&nbsp;

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.680
We start with mess and then we make choices.&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.770
First hundreds of choices and then thousands
of choices.&nbsp;

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:35.040
And before we’re done, tens of thousands
of individual choices have been made.&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.689
But you cannot make choices and you cannot
see what or choose what direction you want

00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:48.470
your story to go in or how you want it to
develop unless you have a pile of words on

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.490
the page.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.220
So we have exercises sometimes, let’s try
it.&nbsp;

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.069
Let’s write as badly as we know how.&nbsp;

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:57.069
And it’s funny.&nbsp;

00:02:57.069 --> 00:02:59.459
You read them aloud and everybody gets a laugh
and this kind of stuff.&nbsp;

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:06.069
And what they find out is…even when they
are trying to write badly (because that is

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:07.069
what we’re doing.&nbsp;

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:10.790
It’s this little exercise) it still comes
out funny.&nbsp;

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.370
It’s not bad in other words.&nbsp;

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.209
They’re funny, they’re cute, they’re
weird, they’re quippy.&nbsp;

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:21.729
But even writing badly, they’re not really
writing that badly at all.

